# agent-evidence-vectors

Labelled conformance cases for AI-agent and supply-chain evidence formats: {cases} cases in {corpora} corpora, each one a record a verifier must accept or reject, with the verdict it must reach.

It is for people who build or evaluate verifiers for agent execution evidence (in-toto attestations, done receipts, audit records, MCP and A2A records, SCITT statements) and for evaluation harnesses that need cases with known answers.

This dataset mirrors release [{tag}]({repository}/releases/tag/{tag}) of [{slug}]({repository}), commit `{commit}`, byte for byte. Pin it by revision:

```python
from datasets import load_dataset

cases = load_dataset("{dataset}", "cases", revision="{tag}", split="cases")
print(cases.num_rows)
print(cases.filter(lambda r: r["corpus"] == "vectors")[0]["expected"])
```

```text
{cases}
{first_expected}
```

Each row names its `corpus`, `suite`, `case_id`, `kind`, the `path` of the vector file in this repository, the `expected` verdict as JSON (null where a corpus keeps the verdict inside the vector file), and the full manifest `entry`. The vector files themselves sit under each corpus directory at the same paths as on GitHub. To run a verifier over them, download the snapshot and check every byte first:

```bash
hf download {dataset} --repo-type dataset --revision {tag} --local-dir aev
cd aev && sha256sum -c --quiet SHA256SUMS && echo all files match
```

## Status

Version {tag}. Each corpus release on GitHub becomes a new revision tag here with the same name. `release/CORPUS-DIGESTS.txt` is the maintainer-signed list of every corpus digest, with an RFC 3161 timestamp and an OpenTimestamps proof over the signature; `SOURCE.json` records the tag, the commit and the checks that passed before upload. A tag can be moved, so a conformance claim cites the commit hash of the revision it ran.

